> Hmm... dogscoff _did_ use valid 7-bit characters, however, his user
> agent ("G2/0.2") mangled them anyway.  For example, "|" (a perfectly
> valid character) was sent as "=A6" (quoted-printable).  The screenshot
> posted to Usenet properly used -+| as line-drawing characters, the point
> of breakage was the nntp client used to post it.

What dogscoff sent was '¦' rather than '|'; it has a break in the middle and
"=A6" is indeed its correct quoted-printable expansion (if anything about
quoted-printable on Usenet can be called "correct").  There were also
several instances of '±'.

That said, KPD's declaration that "Usenet is _still_ a seven bit pure
alphabet medium" is superstition without basis in fact.  Browse any
non-English language newsgroup and you'll see people throwing around
non-ASCII characters without apparent trouble.  Unicode may still not be
safe to use on Usenet, but ISO-8859-1 is here, it's supported by all major
OSes and several minor ones, and it's high time for the ten people still
using non-eight-bit-clean (read "broken") software to catch up to the 1990s
and upgrade.

What's so hard about eight-bit-clean software?  You just pass the eighth bit
unchanged, the same way as the other seven.  You just avoid such
shenanigans as "ch &= 0x7F;".  It's _less_ work to make eight-bit-clean
software than broken crap that strips the eighth bits.  Or am I being
naïve?

Where Google Groups goofed up (besides using quoted-printable) was in
inserting spurious line breaks into the map.  Even a news client that
decodes the quoted-printable and correctly displays the ISO-8859-1
characters in a proper fixed-width font will still show a mangled map. 
Perhaps the quoted-printable triggered some bug in the line break
algorithm.

